# ─── Line endings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# EVERY text file is LF, in the index and in the working tree. This line is
# the whole fix; the rest of the file is exceptions and diff behaviour.
#
# Why a catch-all rather than a list of extensions: the previous version
# pinned eol=lf for *.sh, *.py, *.yml, *.yaml and stopped there. Markdown was
# not covered, so 220 of 289 tracked files carried CRLF in the index, and any
# editor writing LF turned a four-line edit into a whole-file rewrite. A
# A whole-file diff for a four-line change is not reviewable, which defeats
# the point of keeping documents in git at all. Adopters inherit this file,
# so getting it right here fixes it everywhere downstream.
#
# This also overrides core.autocrlf, which is set to true in every local
# clone here. .gitattributes wins over that setting, so behaviour no longer
# depends on how an individual machine is configured — which is the actual
# prevention. A new contributor on Windows gets LF whether or not they know
# what autocrlf is.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; the binary list below removes the
# guesswork for formats where a wrong guess corrupts the file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ─── Binary: never convert, never diff as text ───────────────────────────────
#
# Generated documents and images. Marking them explicitly rather than relying
# on git's heuristic: a rendered DOCX that git decides is "text" gets its
# line endings rewritten and stops opening.
*.pptx  binary
*.docx  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.xlsx  binary

# ─── Prose-friendly diffs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# diff=markdown activates git's BUILT-IN markdown userdiff driver (git >=
# 2.32, no configuration required — verified on git 2.50: hunk headers change
# from a random nearby paragraph line to the enclosing heading, e.g.
# "@@ -53,3 +53,3 @@ ## Prerequisites"). The driver also supplies a
# prose-aware word regex that `git diff --word-diff` picks up automatically.
#
# Word-level diffing itself is per-invocation, not per-attribute:
#   git diff --word-diff                    # {+added+} / [-removed-] inline
#   git diff --word-diff=color              # colored words, no markers
*.md   diff=markdown
*.qmd  diff=markdown

# ─── Diagnosing a file that still misbehaves ─────────────────────────────────
#
# `git ls-files --eol <path>` reports what git actually believes:
#   i/lf   w/lf    correct
#   i/crlf         CRLF is committed; run `git add --renormalize .`
#   i/-text        git classified the file as BINARY, so no eol rule applies
#
# The last one is the trap. An attribute cannot rescue a file git has already
# decided is binary, and the usual cause is a raw control byte embedded in
# the source — a literal CR inside a string, say. Remove the byte first, then
# renormalize; until then the rule above is silently skipped.
